\
## Exploring Ghana's Transition Pathways to Electric 2/3-Wheelers\
\
**Implementing Partner:** Centre for Extractives and Development Africa (CEDA)\
**Funder:** UK Foreign, Commonwealth and Development Office (FCDO), through the Climate Compatible Growth (CCG) Programme via Loughborough University\
**Duration:** March 2025 \'96 February 2027 (22 months)\
**Geographic scope:** Ghana \'97 with primary fieldwork across Greater Accra, Ashanti, Bono East, Northern, and Upper East regions\
\
*[Suggested image: Project banner / inception meeting group photo]*\
\
---\
\
## At a Glance\
\
Ghana stands at a defining moment in its mobility transition. Two- and three-wheelers (motorcycles, tricycles, and the popular *okada* and *aboboyaa*) are the workhorses of informal transport, last-mile delivery, and rural mobility \'97 yet they remain largely absent from the country's electric vehicle policy and climate commitments. CEDA's CCG-funded project is generating the evidence base needed to close that gap.\
\
The project addresses a striking imbalance: between 2017 and 2021, electric two- and three-wheelers (E2&3Ws) accounted for 96% of Ghana's electric vehicle imports, yet national policy continues to focus disproportionately on four-wheeled EVs. Globally, the International Energy Agency projects 490 million E2&3Ws on the world's roads by 2030, more than three times the projected stock of light-duty plug-in electric vehicles. For Ghana, prioritising this segment offers the fastest, most inclusive route to decarbonising transport.\
\
*[Suggested image: Infographic \'97 96% of EV imports / 490m global E2&3W projection]*\
\
