\
## The Challenge\
\
Ghana's National Electric Vehicle Policy (2023), the National Transport Policy (2020), and the Updated Nationally Determined Contributions (2020\'962030) all acknowledge the role of sustainable mobility \'97 but none provide a clear, definitive pathway for electric two- and three-wheelers. Motorcycle taxis, until recently technically illegal, were only formally legalised under the Road Traffic Amendment Bill 2025. This long-standing regulatory ambiguity has held back investment, distorted financing options, and excluded riders from the protections and support afforded to other transport workers.\
\
The transition is further complicated by structural barriers. Distribution-network constraints \'97 including nearly 1,000 transformers operating beyond rated capacity \'97 pose risks for clustered charging in urban centres. Charging infrastructure remains thin, with fewer than ten public stations operating nationally as of 2024. Skills gaps are pronounced: 100% of surveyed informal mechanics lack formal EV training, and women make up under 1% of the informal repair workforce. Without deliberate policy design, Ghana risks a transition that reproduces existing inequalities rather than redressing them.\
\
