\
## Headline Findings to Date\
\
**Economics favour the switch.** Primary survey evidence and TCO modelling show that electric two- and three-wheelers are economically competitive with petrol counterparts under realistic operating conditions, even without government subsidies. The SolarTaxi ST-04 electric motorcycle delivers a five-year total cost of ownership 27% lower than the equivalent petrol motorcycle at 20,000 km/year, with the break-even point reached at approximately 12,000\'9613,000 km annually.\
\
**Local manufacturing potential is real but underdeveloped.** Ghana's E2&3W manufacturing sector currently comprises five to seven formal actors employing about 150 workers directly, with the broader ecosystem supporting 3,000\'965,000 livelihoods. Under ambitious policy scenarios, five-year employment projections rise to between 7,500 and 11,500 jobs. Frames, wiring harnesses, seats and plastics are immediately localisable; battery pack assembly and motors are medium-to-long-term opportunities.\
\
**Grid readiness is the binding infrastructure constraint.** Generation capacity (over 5,000 MW installed) is sufficient in the short to medium term. The challenge sits in the distribution network \'97 particularly in Greater Accra and Kumasi \'97 where overloaded feeders and aging transformers risk localised stress from clustered commercial charging. Northern Ghana's strong solar resource makes it well-suited to off-grid and hybrid charging hubs.\
\
**Inclusion cannot be assumed.** Without deliberate design, the transition risks reinforcing existing inequalities. Women, persons with disabilities, low-income riders, and rural users face distinct barriers to access, financing, and employment in the sector \'97 barriers that policy and infrastructure design must explicitly address.\
