\
## Why It Matters\
\
The project's contribution sits at the intersection of climate, economic, and social policy. Successful electrification of two- and three-wheelers can deliver immediate emissions reductions, lower household and operator costs, expand green employment across assembly, servicing and charging, and improve mobility for groups currently underserved by the formal transport system. By generating evidence in time to inform Ghana's NDC update, the National Energy Transition Framework, and the implementing regulations of the Road Traffic Amendment Bill 2025, the project is positioned to shape policy at a moment when the regulatory environment is genuinely open to change.\
\
Beyond Ghana, the work has regional resonance. With comparable transport patterns across West Africa, an evidence-based, gender-responsive transition pathway developed in Ghana can serve as a reference model for neighbouring countries facing similar choices.\
